"texte": [
"In-Depth Interview — Conversation with Dr. Nadia Okafor, Professor of Social Policy and Urban Inequality — Journalist: Traditionally, social exclusion was understood primarily in terms of material deprivation—lack of income, unemployment, or inadequate housing. While economic poverty remains acute, your recent research suggests that twenty-first-century societies are generating subtler, multidimensional forms of exclusion. What defines these new mechanisms of marginalization?",
"Dr. Nadia Okafor: Contemporary exclusion rarely operates through explicit legal prohibitions; instead, it functions through invisible infrastructural, digital, and spatial thresholds that quietly shut people out of full civic participation. Consider the rapid digitalization of essential services. When banking, healthcare appointments, tax filings, and public transit ticketing migrate almost exclusively onto smartphone applications, millions of elderly citizens, low-income households without reliable broadband, and individuals with limited digital literacy are effectively disenfranchised from everyday life. They are not formally denied their rights, yet the practical pathways to exercising those rights have been dismantled.",
"Journalist: Beyond the digital divide, how are geographic and environmental transformations reshaping patterns of exclusion?",
"Dr. Nadia Okafor: We are witnessing a profound spatial sorting of opportunity. In many metropolitan regions, skyrocketing housing costs have pushed essential service workers—nurses, teachers, sanitation staff, retail employees—into poorly connected outer peripheries. Cut off from reliable public transit, quality schools, and cultural institutions, these communities experience what urbanists call 'territorial relegation.' Simultaneously, environmental inequality has emerged as a powerful driver of exclusion: disadvantaged neighborhoods are disproportionately exposed to urban heat islands, air pollution, and flood risks while lacking access to green spaces, compounding health disparities across generations.",
"Journalist: You also write about 'algorithmic and credential exclusion' in labor markets and public institutions. How do these less visible barriers operate?",
"Dr. Nadia Okafor: Automated screening tools used in hiring, credit scoring, and tenant selection frequently encode historical biases embedded in training data, silently filtering out applicants from marginalized postal codes or non-traditional educational backgrounds without any human accountability. Meanwhile, the inflation of formal academic credentials increasingly excludes capable, experienced workers from stable careers simply because they lack expensive university degrees. Combined with precarious gig-economy contracts that strip away sick leave and pension security, these mechanisms create a tier of second-class economic citizenship.",
"Journalist: What policy shifts are required to counteract these interlocking forms of exclusion?",
"Dr. Nadia Okafor: Combating contemporary exclusion demands designing universal accessibility into the very architecture of society. That means guaranteeing human-staffed public service alternatives alongside digital platforms, investing in affordable housing and rapid transit to reconnect segregated territories, regulating algorithmic decision-making for transparency and fairness, and valuing diverse forms of practical skill alongside academic degrees. True social inclusion cannot be achieved through charity after the fact; it requires building institutions that leave no one stranded at the threshold."
